# Constructing a cube from its description

This paper describes one cube so exactly that any reader who follows it builds the same object, written
the same way. A reader turns the description into a list of corner points and faces; the kernel then
checks the end state. The checks admit exactly one record, so every answer the kernel accepts is
identical, whoever wrote it. Readers who cannot reach that record are rejected rather than accepted with
a different cube: the measured part is how many readers reach acceptance.

## Definitions

- **coordinate system**: right-handed x, y and z axes meeting at the origin (0, 0, 0); every length is in units
- **vertex**: a corner point of the solid, written as [x, y, z]
- **face**: a flat side of the solid, written as the numbers of its vertices in counter-clockwise order seen from outside the solid
- **edge length**: the distance between two vertices joined by an edge
- **canonical numbering**: vertices numbered from 1 in ascending order of x, then y, then z; each face starts at its smallest vertex number; faces sorted in ascending order, comparing the first number, then the second

## The object

The solid is a cube. Every edge is 2 units long. The centre of the cube is the origin. Every face is
perpendicular to the x axis, the y axis or the z axis. It follows that every coordinate of every vertex
is 1 or -1, so the cube has 8 vertices and 6 square faces of 4 vertices each.

```symblic
define Shape as Record {vertices: List of (List of Number length 3) length 8, faces: List of (List of Int length 4) length 6}.
```

## Construction

The reader writes the cube's vertices and faces using the canonical numbering. Nothing else is needed.

```symblic
ask cube_solid: Shape "Construct the cube this paper describes. Give its 8 vertices and 6 faces using the canonical numbering." with retries=3
```

## End state

The kernel accepts the construction only if every check holds: it is a closed solid, it is a cube with
edges 2 units long, every coordinate is 1 or -1, and it is written in the canonical numbering. Together
these admit exactly one record.

```symblic
ensure solid_check(cube_solid).valid_solid because solid_check(cube_solid).problems.
ensure is_cube(cube_solid, 2) because "the solid must be a cube with every edge 2 units long".
ensure all p in cube_solid.vertices: all c in p: c = 1 or c = -1 because "the cube is centred at the origin with faces perpendicular to the axes, so every coordinate is 1 or -1".
ensure cube_solid = canonical_shape(cube_solid) because "use the canonical numbering: vertices by x, then y, then z; faces counter-clockwise from outside, starting at the smallest number, sorted".
```

## Result

The accepted cube, its fingerprint and its drawing are computed by the kernel.

```symblic
show hash(cube_solid) as "cube fingerprint".
show shape_facts(cube_solid).volume as "volume".
draw draw_shape(cube_solid) as "cube.svg".
```
